{
  "id": "Compound::DB00091 - Gene::1557",
  "verbalization": "Cyclosporine is implicated in the regulation of Cyp2c19 through multiple mechanistic pathways, with the core mechanism involving the inhibition of cytochrome P450 2c19 (Cyp2c19), a key enzyme in drug metabolism. The evidence suggests that Cyclosporine may act as a substrate or inhibitor of Cyp2c19, as it is linked to the induction of hyperamylasaemia, joint dislocation, and pustular psoriasis, all of which are associated with Cyp2c19 activity. The binding of Cyclosporine to Cyp2c19 is supported by the presence of a direct interaction in the visible paths, although the exact nature of this interaction remains unclear. The evidence also highlights that Cyclosporine is associated with immunotherapeutic agents and organic amino compounds, which may provide additional context for its role in modulating Cyp2c19 activity. The integration of these pathways suggests that Cyclosporine exerts its effects on Cyp2c19 through a mechanism that involves either direct inhibition or modulation of enzyme activity, potentially influencing drug metabolism and therapeutic outcomes.",
